UID:4b9ed0ce1e02be2b@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260807T191257Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260807
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Expanding Local Food Production and Agricultu
 re Education Across Maine
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nWell\, you know\, one of the lessons learned from the pandemic time
  was how dependent we are as a state and community on producers from other
  states and other countries for basic goods like food\, textiles\, and con
 struction materials. It occurred to me that our growing season is too incr
 edibly short for us to become as self-reliant as we should be.\nAnd after 
 the pandemic disrupted markets and caused food shortages nationwide\, we r
 ealized it was very important to have locally grown\, fresh food right her
 e in Maine and help our local farmers. So I used federal funding through m
 y Jobs and Recovery Plan to provide grants to farmers to buy equipment to 
 meet that higher demand for locally grown food\, and I began to look for o
 ther ways to extend our growing season here in Maine.\nWell\, one of the t
 hings you can do is to provide greenhouses to communities who are able to 
 support them and help people of all ages learn how to grow their own food.
  I called on the Legislature to provide one-time funds through the state b
 iennial budget to buy greenhouses for as many schools and communities as p
 ossible. That will strengthen our supply of local food and will teach kids
  and families to grow their own food and extend the growing season.\nThe L
 egislature funded that proposal\, and I'm proud to announce that we're now
  awarding grants to communities across Maine to expand their local food pr
 oduction and education programs through durable shared-use greenhouses. Th
 ese grants were awarded through a competitive process. The organizations a
 pplied for them\, and they include the Alfond Youth and Community Center i
 n Waterville\, where they're going to install a greenhouse to expand local
  food production and youth education.\nThe Healthy Island Project in Stoni
 ngton will use a greenhouse to increase access to fresh\, locally grown fo
 od. And the Portland Community Squash will install a greenhouse to send mo
 re fresh produce home to low-inco\nhttp://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/new
 s/radio_address/expanding-local-food-production-and-agriculture-education-
 across-maine-2026-08
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/expanding-local
 -food-production-and-agriculture-education-across-maine-2026-08
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
